course: dictionary
lesson: 146
title: Showing affection
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Affection is a gentle feeling of liking and caring for someone or something. It is also the way you show that care, with hugs, kind words or friendly actions.
explain: «You can show affection your own way, such as a high-five or a wave instead of a kiss.» The clue is "your own way": affection does not have to be a hug or a kiss, and it can be a small action that shows you care.
explain: «Families often tease about funny moments; it usually means affection, but it can still sting.» Teasing can come from affection, yet a person may still feel hurt. Notice how someone feels, and always check that a hug or joke is welcome before you give it.
fact: Affection is a noun, and it is uncountable, so we say "a lot of affection", not "many affections".
fact: Affections is a plural form, used mostly in formal writing, for many feelings of affection.
fact: Useful phrases are «show affection», «with affection» and «a sign of affection».
fact: Affection can be shown without touching: a wave, a smile, a high-five or kind words all count.
fact: Always ask before a hug. Affection is only kind when the other person is happy to receive it.
exercise: «You can show affection your own way, such as a high-five or a wave instead of a kiss.» Which of these is a way to show affection? | A loud shout across the playground | Hiding a friend's pencil | *A wave to a friend | A slammed door
exercise: Affection means what? | A loud noise in a shop | A rule that everyone must follow | *A gentle feeling of liking and caring for someone or something | Sharp anger at someone who has annoyed you
exercise: «Families often tease about funny moments; it usually means affection, but it can still sting.» What does this tell you? | Teasing is always cruel and never kind | The family is angry with each other | Teasing is only for grown-ups | *Teasing can come from affection and still hurt a little
test: Which word is a noun that means a gentle feeling of caring? | *Affection | Affect | Affectionately | Affectionate
test: Which sentence uses affection correctly? | Affection the cat was very loud. | He was affection quickly to the bus. | *She showed her affection with a warm hug. | The dog affection me at the park.
exam: Which is a good way to show affection to a friend who does not like hugs? | Ignore them until they ask | Hug them anyway, because affection always means a hug | *Give a high-five or a wave, and ask before anything more | Shout their name across the room
